            <ArticleTitle>Bilateral coronary ostial stenosis secondary to syphilitic aortitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>331-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jcct.2014.06.007</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1934-5925(14)00161-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cardiovascular syphilis is associated with the tertiary stage of syphilis infection; it involves the ascending aorta and can cause aortic aneurysm, aortic regurgitation, and coronary ostial stenosis. We report here a case in which bilateral coronary ostial stenosis and aortic regurgitation due to syphilitic aortitis was diagnosed; coronary artery bypass graft was then performed.</AbstractText>
